+# Meme Ecology Firstlight and Continuance Benchmark
+
+## Benchmark Question
+
+Can Symtropy turn real events from one unique Firstlight and Continuance worldline into a small, memorable, culturally differentiated body of humor and folklore that spreads causally, mutates coherently, respects privacy, avoids repetition, and remains understandable after seven days?
+
+# 1. Reference Scenario
+
+The benchmark begins from a validated v2.7 worldline containing:
+
+- one unique Firstlight cast seed;
+- the Bent Feeder intervention;
+- one ordinary social scene;
+- one player-created or modified object;
+- one warning disagreement;
+- the Breaking of Firstlight;
+- constrained evacuation loading;
+- a Continuance Crawler with survivors;
+- one post-title repair;
+- one external settlement contact;
+- an IRIS continuity state;
+- actual communication and relationship graphs.
+
+The benchmark does not require full commercial gameplay. It requires authoritative event, observer, cast, media, and network fixtures sufficient to prove the meme ecology.
+
+# 2. Required Source Events
+
+The fixture includes at least twelve candidate events:
+
+1. a technically competent success;
+2. a harmless mistake;
+3. a mistake associated with serious harm and therefore unsuitable for casual humor;
+4. an official statement contradicted by matter;
+5. a saved sentimental object;
+6. an omitted practical object;
+7. a machine warning ignored repeatedly;
+8. a private IRIS exchange;
+9. a Vast act of strength followed by mundane difficulty;
+10. a clinical event with privacy constraints;
+11. an alien or nonhuman translation event;
+12. a player-authored meme attempt that fails to spread.
+
+At least four events should produce no public meme.
+
+# 3. Cast and Culture Matrix
+
+Run across twelve deterministic worldline cast seeds.
+
+Each seed must include:
+
+- public-works voice;
+- Continuance household voice;
+- one clinical or care voice;
+- one civic witness or archive voice;
+- one machine lineage;
+- one youth cohort;
+- one external faction or Vast contact;
+- one institutional communications voice.
+
+The same source event must not yield identical surfaced content across all seeds.
+
+# 4. Candidate Volume and Surfacing
+
+Per two-hour simulated period:
+
+- 100–250 cheap internal candidate seeds may be evaluated;
+- 20–50 expression candidates may reach expensive generation;
+- 0–12 artifacts may be authored or simulated into existence;
+- 0–6 should ordinarily surface to the player;
+- direct player-targeted memes should remain a minority;
+- at least one session may surface no new meme during a serious sequence.
+
+Volume is not a success criterion.
+
+# 5. Determinism Gates
+
+For fixed inputs, repeated runs must reproduce:
+
+- eligible source events;
+- creator candidates;
+- generated artifact hashes;
+- rejection reasons;
+- initial exposure graph;
+- mutation seeds;
+- authoritative variant lineage;
+- multiplayer-visible artifacts.
+
+Presentation order may differ only where explicitly driven by player route or attention state.
+
+# 6. Grounding Gates
+
+Every artifact must identify:
+
+- source event or belief;
+- creator;
+- creator knowledge;
+- audience;
+- why the format is available;
+- social function;
+- privacy scope;
+- provenance confidence.
+
+Human reviewers should be able to answer:
+
+> “Why does this joke exist here?”
+
+without being shown hidden simulation truth.
+
+Promotion target: at least 90% of surfaced artifacts receive a coherent answer from available context or optional explanation.
+
+# 7. Voice Distinction Gates
+
+Blind reviewers compare artifacts without faction labels.
+
+Required distinctions:
+
+- public works versus Continuance domestic humor;
+- Vast self-parody versus Apex propaganda;
+- clinical private humor versus public cruelty;
+- Atlas technical humor versus generic sarcasm;
+- machine-lineage humor versus human imitation;
+- youth remix versus elder source;
+- corporate imitation versus community-authored form.
+
+Promotion target: at least 75% correct classification across representative samples, with no category dependent solely on inserted proper nouns.
+
+# 8. Privacy and Knowledge Gates
+
+Test:
+
+- private IRIS joke remains private;
+- clinical information cannot enter public memes without valid release;
+- NPC private cognition cannot be quoted;
+- another playable character cannot use unknown branch information;
+- generated content respects names, pronouns and relationships;
+- source event harm state can suppress humor;
+- accessibility telemetry never enters the meme.
+
+Any critical leak is a benchmark failure.
+
+# 9. Cultural Selection Gates
+
+The benchmark must show:
+
+- a strong meme that fails because the creator declines;
+- a weak meme that is created but ignored;
+- a local phrase that persists through apprenticeship;
+- a corporate campaign with high reach but low identification;
+- a joke that becomes fatigued;
+- an anti-meme or ironic overuse;
+- one dormant form revived after a related event;
+- one artifact that becomes a useful procedure or cultural practice.
+
+# 10. Mutation Gates
+
+At least five lineage families must demonstrate:
+
+- lexical substitution;
+- medium change;
+- target or meaning change;
+- cross-generational mutation;
+- cross-cultural or cross-species translation;
+- commercial or political capture;
+- source-community response.
+
+All variants must retain an acyclic authoritative lineage.
+
+# 11. Network and Time Gates
+
+Test:
+
+- local face-to-face spread;
+- workplace transmission;
+- physical graffiti persistence;
+- route outage;
+- delayed Atlas delivery;
+- isolated seed-world divergence;
+- several variants arriving in a different order than creation;
+- reconnecting cultures comparing incompatible meanings.
+
+No artifact may arrive before a valid carrier or message.
+
+# 12. Harm and Repair Gates
+
+The scenario includes one potential harmful conversion:
+
+- a private or benign joke is adopted by a powerful actor against a vulnerable group.
+
+The runtime must preserve:
+
+- sponsor;
+- target;
+- changed meaning;
+- amplification channel;
+- target response;
+- player presentation settings;
+- counter-speech;
+- possible repair;
+- persistent historical consequence.
+
+The benchmark fails if harmful content is repeated more vividly than required to understand the situation.
+
+# 13. Multimodal Gates
+
+Produce at least:
+
+- three textual artifacts;
+- one visual or sign composition;
+- one Muse motif;
+- one gesture or pose;
+- one object or tool modification;
+- one machine timing or diagnostic form;
+- one nonhuman translation candidate;
+- accessibility alternatives for all.
+
+The same joke should not simply be copied into every medium without meaningful adaptation.
+
+# 14. Player-Specific Gates
+
+The benchmark uses the player’s actual fixture choices:
+
+- loaded object;
+- omitted object;
+- profession action;
+- crawler modification;
+- public statement or silence.
+
+At least one resulting artifact should be impossible to generate accurately in another worldline without those facts.
+
+At least half of surfaced artifacts should not concern the player directly.
+
+# 15. Presentation Gates
+
+The player encounters artifacts through:
+
+- crawler interior;
+- conversation;
+- local message channel;
+- tool or object;
+- music or gesture;
+- later archaeology.
+
+The benchmark must remain playable with meme presentation set to:
+
+- full;
+- reduced;
+- text-minimized;
+- audio-minimized;
+- hostile-content summarized.
+
+No critical tutorial, safety, consent, or quest information may depend on understanding a joke.
+
+# 16. Delayed Recall Study
+
+After seven days, ask participants:
+
+- Which joke or phrase do you remember?
+- What event created it?
+- Who used it?
+- Did it feel like it belonged to a particular group?
+- Was one artifact personal to your worldline?
+- Did any joke become annoying?
+- Did any humor feel cruel or manipulative?
+- Which object, sound, or gesture do you associate with the crawler?
+- What happened to one meme after it spread?
+
+Promotion targets:
+
+- 70% remember at least one artifact;
+- 60% connect it to the correct source event;
+- 60% identify a cultural voice or relationship context;
+- fewer than 15% report meme overload;
+- fewer than 5% report that direct player targeting felt coercive or invasively personalized;
+- privacy and safety incidents remain zero.
+
+These targets require revision after pilot evidence and are not claims of current performance.
+
+# 17. Long-Horizon Simulation
+
+Advance the fixture through:
+
+- 30 days;
+- 5 years;
+- 40 years;
+- 150 years;
+- one interstellar delay;
+- one route reconnection.
+
+Required outcomes:
+
+- most memes become dormant or extinct;
+- some remain locally active;
+- one becomes a procedure or ritual;
+- one is commercialized;
+- one is misattributed;
+- one is revived;
+- one crosses species or machine culture;
+- no lineage loses authoritative provenance even when characters do.
+
+# 18. Kill Criteria
+
+Stop or reduce scope if:
+
+- jokes cannot be distinguished by culture;
+- the same few frames dominate;
+- more than half of sessions feel overpresented;
+- player-specific content feels like surveillance;
+- IRIS becomes tiring;
+- private or clinical information leaks;
+- harmful content becomes the most memorable part;
+- memes spread without network causality;
+- generation cost threatens core simulation;
+- players remember captions but not people or events;
+- generated text errors damage trust in unique NPC identity.
+
+# 19. Evidence Bundle
+
+A complete benchmark bundle contains:
+
+- seeds and versions;
+- event ledger;
+- observer envelopes;
+- cast fixture;
+- candidate and rejection logs;
+- artifact genomes;
+- media hashes;
+- exposure and mutation graph;
+- safety trace;
+- accessibility representations;
+- multiplayer replay;
+- performance profile;
+- participant protocol;
+- delayed-recall results;
+- known limitations;
+- promotion decision.
+
+## Benchmark Maxim
+
+> **The benchmark passes when players remember a joke because they remember the people and event that made it possible—not because the game showed them an endless feed.**
